ial/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-79200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=0"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-79200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-3$ and $x=4$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-114418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-114418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-114418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f8b8d8b2405723c2f603029d31a3d29a1b167dfb079504c8943f479686dcc2f"} {"question" "q-114418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-114418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$g(x)$ has no inflection points"] ["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=\\pm 1$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-114418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$g(x)$ has no inflection points"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**,the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)+{e}^{x}(2x-12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22)+{e}^{x}(2x-10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=1,\\,$ $\\,x=4,\\,$ and $\\,x=7,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1-2)(1-6))=5e>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4-2)(4-6))=-4{e}^{4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(7) & ={e}^{7}((7-2)(7-6))=5{e}^{7}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2$ and $x=6$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6,$ we conclude that $x=2$ and $x=6$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=2$ and $x=6$ are inflection points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f8b8d8b2405723c2f603029d31a3d29a1b167dfb079504c8943f479686dcc2f"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"} {"question" "q-344301" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e33a44bcbe3259e2679c784c8e9e566efcbd1da06c3ccd0ba85d0cbcdd545d"}] "usage" {"q-79200" [] "q-21035" [] "q-114418" []} "basis" 2504 "retractions" [[17592186294077 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nTh